WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:06.170
- Welcome everyone to the
Refugee Migration Workshop.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.290
I'm Steven Wilkinson, I'm the director

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:14.250
of the MacMillan Center within
which the program is located.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.550
It gives me my very great pleasure today

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.410
to welcome my friend and colleague,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:20.943
Professor Nicholas Sambanis,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.580
to present on his work on
overcoming discrimination.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.260
Professor Sambanis is a leading authority

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.790
in international relations.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:37.050
He's written really
foundational works on civil war

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.330
and peace and conflict

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.780
and intervening to preserve peace.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.490
And more recently,

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.240
his interests have also
moved towards looking at

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.550
domestic politics and
overcoming discrimination

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.000
around the world, and
especially in Europe.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.040
And that's what he's gonna
be speaking on today.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.697
And he's recently published this book,

00:00:57.697 --> 00:01:00.780
"Native Bias: Overcoming
Discrimination against Immigrants"

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.220
which as we know is timely,
really everywhere in the world.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.880
So we welcome also the people
who are watching online.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.542
You can submit questions in the chat

00:01:12.542 --> 00:01:15.690
and have a chance to
have Professor Sambanis

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.940
answer those in the Q and A.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.953
So, welcome Professor Sambanis.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.163
- Thank you, thank you very
much for this invitation.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.450
I was asked to present this book

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:37.050
that I was coauthored with Danny Choi

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.950
and Mathias Poertner,

00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:43.980
who are postdoctoral fellows at my lab,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.170
the Identity &amp; Conflict Lab at Penn

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.817
and now assistant professors
at Brown and LLC respectively.

00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:54.990
And the topic of the conversation today

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.160
and of the book is on strategies

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.860
to reduce native bias against immigrants,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.080
Muslim immigrants, in
particular in Europe.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.250
And the title of the
presentation, as you can see,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.410
is, Can Shared Norms of Good Citizenship

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.060
Reduce Native-Immigrant Conflict?

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.020
And we're motivated to study this topic

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.770
by the observations that
are being made constantly

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.680
around Europe, that natives are actually

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.970
opposed to immigration.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.253
You see in your screen here,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.500
data from several countries in
Europe with people responding

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.610
to the question that there are

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.110
so many immigrants living here

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.940
that it doesn't feel like home anymore.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.400
And you see that in Italy,
France, and Germany, for example.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.910
About half of the population
agrees with that statement.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.140
In Greece, where I'm from, 85%

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.960
of the population in a
survey that was done,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.470
I believe in 2020, would
agree with the statement

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.930
that the number of immigrants is too large

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.890
and the percentage agreeing
among the young people,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.890
age 17 to 34, who are usually
more liberal, more accepting

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.863
than older people, is 70%, which is huge.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.203
And 56% of the population believe

00:03:22.203 --> 00:03:24.600
that immigrants are actually negative,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.480
have a negative impact on
the economy, which is strange

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.610
because there are entire
sectors of the Greek economy

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.340
that just do not operate
without migrant labor like

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.343
agriculture, tourism, and
construction for example.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.630
And 84% prefer all refugees who arrived

00:03:41.896 --> 00:03:45.573
at the 2015 refugee
crisis to leave Greece.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.710
These are pretty staggering
numbers suggesting

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.510
hostility towards immigrants.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.300
And one of the objectives of this book

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.130
and of the broader project that
I'll talk to you about today

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.200
is to uncover the causes of immigrant bias

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.670
and then to consider potential

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.050
strategies to reduce that, right?

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.190
So there is a very large
literature in political science

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.490
on this topic, particularly
on causes of native bias

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.850
and causes of prejudice in
general towards all kinds

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.643
of minority groups, not just immigrants.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.970
And I've organized them here in a list of,

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.910
so the three dominant explanations are,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:31.910
one, that it might be racist
and just xenophobia prejudice

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.050
towards any group that is not part

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.630
of the ingroup and is
culturally or different

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.637
in respect to any kind of characteristics.

00:04:39.637 --> 00:04:42.520
And there is some evidence
of this both with respect

00:04:43.511 --> 00:04:46.740
to immigrant groups, but
also other minority groups.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.490
A second very popular explanation is

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.750
that economic competition between natives

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.390
and immigrants is what explains

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.740
hostility towards immigrants.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.290
You know, this is a
very popular explanation

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.580
in the United States and also in Europe.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.490
But interestingly, there
is very little evidence

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.790
to support this, you know,
from cross-country studies.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.810
So the least likely
explanation is that one,

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.930
as far as the literature
is concerned at the moment.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.100
And then the third

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.840
and the most likely explanation for

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.820
what causes bias against immigrants is

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.880
that cultural differences
between the native population

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.113
and the immigrant groups create
the perception of threat.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.630
And that is what motivates natives

00:05:35.664 --> 00:05:37.140
to react against immigration

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.150
because they perceive immigrants

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.640
as potentially changing the
national identity, right?

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.170
So they see the symbols

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.990
of cultural difference of
immigrants in this picture here,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.450
it's the hijab that these
women are wearing as a symbol

00:05:53.524 --> 00:05:58.524
of change that is unwelcome
in native society.

00:06:05.581 --> 00:06:08.451
The theory that we develop in the book

00:06:08.451 --> 00:06:11.160
is grounded in social
psychological theories

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.020
of intergroup conflict
that are not specific

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.310
to native immigrant relations,

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.007
but we extend those to some extent

00:06:17.007 --> 00:06:19.653
and we apply insights
from these literatures.

00:06:20.912 --> 00:06:23.970
And what I have here in
bold is the statement

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:27.000
that conflict over waves
of life is at the heart

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.420
of opposition to
multiculturalism in Europe.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:34.020
That's I think the theoretical perspective

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.230
that has received the most
support in survey based

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.620
and experimental research

00:06:39.891 --> 00:06:41.607
in political science in Europe.

00:06:41.607 --> 00:06:44.970
And the reason is that natives are priors

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.730
and stereotypes about the
extent to which immigrants live

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.950
by different rules and different norms,

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.390
and also the extent

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.560
to which these norms are just
different from their own.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.570
And that generates social distance

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.010
between natives and indigenous, right?

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.880
So the larger that social
distance, the larger the bias.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.957
And that's an insight that
comes out of the literature

00:07:06.957 --> 00:07:10.830
and social psychology
that I mentioned earlier.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.590
And "Native Bias", the book
that I'm presenting today

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.740
tests the power of shared norms and ideas

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.470
to reduce expressions of
native immigrant hostility

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.110
by reducing the perception
of social distance

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:23.523
separating these groups.

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:26.700
And in an ongoing, the
book is out already,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.670
so I'll present the empirical
results from the book,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.130
but I also at the end
discuss some extensions

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.563
that I have ongoing currently, right?

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.910
So I mentioned that the theory,
the theoretical foundation

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.780
of the book lies in social
psychology literature

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.820
that goes back decades in
particular social identity theory

00:07:47.119 --> 00:07:50.910
and which has taught us
that any kind of descriptive

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.670
or other group based
difference can generate bias

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.640
even if those differences
are not felt deeply

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.630
and they don't have baggage.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:00.210
So we can create artificial
groups in the lab

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.090
and assign people to these
groups, and that will generate

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:06.783
hostility and competition
between the two groups.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.700
And a lot of that literature
has then gone on to try

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.560
to figure out ways to reduce bias

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:15.850
and reduce conflict between groups

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:21.870
and expression versions of those arguments

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.940
have found their way in political science.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.700
And there are some arguments
out there about ways

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.481
to reduce conflict between
natives and immigrants

00:08:31.481 --> 00:08:35.220
that require immigrants
to assimilate completely

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.840
to native society by shedding the markers

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:40.710
of the difference, right?

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:44.850
So assimilating by
adapting names that are not

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:49.170
visibly foreign, for example,
or are changing the religion.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.450
So these forms of coercive assimilation

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.170
are juxtaposed to the types of strategies

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.760
that we're considering in this book,

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.890
which are far less coercive

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:06.060
and which consist of
converging on a set of norms

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:10.620
of good citizenship
that if applied, if used

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.000
and implemented in everyday
settings, both by natives

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.170
and immigrants, can generate
the perception that everyone

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:23.070
is part of a common
ingroup as citizen ingroup.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.820
And by doing so, that
should reduce the import

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.350
of descriptive markers

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.470
and cultural differences
that can persist, right?

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:33.810
So in other words,

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.810
if immigrants behave in
ways that natives perceived

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.520
as not threatening vis-a-vis norms

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:44.130
and values that they share,

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:48.540
then they should no longer
care about any visible markers

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:51.960
of difference, racial, ethnic, religious,

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.300
or other markers that
immigrants might have.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.930
And therefore there should be
less pressure for immigrants

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.180
to assimilate by shedding those markers.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.350
That's an idea here.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.930
And by the way, you can
jump in with questions

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.880
anytime you'd like in the presentation.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.850
So the data for this book
comes from experiments

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:16.850
that we run in Germany
between 2018 and 2019

00:10:18.941 --> 00:10:19.800
and for the field experiments

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.260
and expanded later with
some survey experiments

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.150
in 2020 and 2021.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.350
We chose Germany because
it was one of two countries

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:31.785
that were most affected by
the refugee crisis of 2015.

00:10:31.785 --> 00:10:35.370
The other one being Greece
and Germany received

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.460
more than one and a half
million applications for asylum

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.780
after the Syrian refugee crisis.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:47.700
And over a long period,
even prior to 2015,

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.670
there was growing opposition
to multiculturalism.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.670
But it's not the most
anti-immigrant nation in Europe,

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:57.870
Italy and Hungary probably

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.960
hold that distinction right now, at least.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.520
Germany is about in the middle.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.340
Like if you look at indices of opposition

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.140
to multiculturalism in Europe,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.830
Germany is is roughly
in the middle, right?

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.830
So it's a large country that's important

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:17.610
and has a very large
population of migrants

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.460
going back generations
since the post-war period

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.293
when they had the worker program.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.540
And so it has had a lot of experience

00:11:27.716 --> 00:11:29.580
with foreign populations

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.350
and it was hit really hard
in this recent crisis.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:37.020
Therefore, it's a good case study.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.670
And what we did to generate
data for this project is

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:43.320
that we created what we call

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.350
microenvironments of social interactions

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:48.780
designed to observe the
degree of assistance

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.310
that is offered to strangers

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:51.810
in everyday interactions, right?

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:55.440
So we focus on everyday settings
in which people interact

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.273
with each other

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.210
because we feel that
this gives us a glimpse

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:07.910
into the types of information

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.623
that people receive about others,

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:15.640
information about the extent
to which one is accepted

00:12:16.898 --> 00:12:18.300
or excluded in society, right?

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.860
So the idea is that

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:26.070
if you observe a stranger
offering you help

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.880
in one instance, and this
happens many, many times

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.930
over time, that will shape
your perceptions of the degree

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:38.930
to which you're accepted as
an equal in that society.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.462
So these everyday settings
that were analyzed

00:12:42.462 --> 00:12:45.883
in this project are

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:48.930
interactions in train stations,

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.930
actually platforms when
people wait the train

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:53.760
to go to work or travel.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:58.200
Germany has many large cities
with many large stations

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:02.940
and there are stations with
like 20, 30, 21 or 24 platforms.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.270
So, and people just gather there

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:09.870
and there's a constant
stream of new people who sit

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.300
for a while and then leave.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.590
And that gives us access
to a lot of bystanders

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.470
whose behavior we can
observe in real time,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:23.650
in a real setting after
exposing them to an experiment

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.980
and intervention that they do not know

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.320
is artificial and is set up by us.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:35.520
So I'm gonna present two
experiments of this sort here,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.040
plus a bunch of survey
experiments that we did

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.620
to tease apart specific mechanisms

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.990
that can explain the results
from a field experiments.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:47.130
The first one consists
of a stage violation

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:48.870
of the norm against literary,

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.210
which Germans care a lot about.

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:54.480
Actually, another reason
why Germany is a good case

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.490
for this analysis is

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.890
because they care a
lot about rules, right?

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:02.850
So rule following is a norm
in its own right no matter

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.560
what kind of work.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.920
So if you ride the bike at night
in Germany without a light,

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.010
people will scream at you, everyone.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.960
If you cross the street,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.446
people will yell at you from
both sides of the street.

00:14:15.446 --> 00:14:17.790
If you have a child while
you scream across the street,

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:19.840
they can scream even more, you know, so

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.090
it's a good test case.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.170
And at the end of the talk,

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:26.820
I'm gonna compare it a
little bit to Greece,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.890
which is precisely the opposite.

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:30.390
People in general don't follow norms.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:31.950
They don't really care if you follow norms

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.840
and no one is gonna intervene to make sure

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.390
that you follow the norm.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:40.600
So we have a multi-country study
that we were developing now

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.630
with (indistinct) who
is post-doc associate

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.210
with my lab, in an economist based Greece

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:53.210
and Chris (indistinct),
professor at Kings College,

00:14:54.206 --> 00:14:58.710
that makes cross country
comparisons of the sort

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.440
that I'm gonna talk to you about today.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.870
But today I will only focus on Germany.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.680
So I'm gonna present the setup

00:15:06.629 --> 00:15:08.040
of the experiment really briefly.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:10.200
I'll show you some footage
and I'll show you the results

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:11.800
and then I'll move on to the next one.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.160
So in this experiment we care

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.380
about these guys, experimental subjects,

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.720
which are basically
ordinary Germans waiting

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.210
in train station to get
on a train, basically.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.310
They can be standing, they can be sitting.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:28.320
And a member of our team,
always male will come in

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:32.190
and violate the norm against
littering by dropping a cup,

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.350
paper cup, with coffee,

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.480
doesn't have coffee in it,
but he is just finished it.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.580
We'll drop it on the platform.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:41.880
And then another member of
that team, always a woman

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:45.360
will in some cases
intervene to tell this guy

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.103
to pick up his trash basically.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:53.913
And please place the paper in a trashcan,

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:59.070
thereby signaling to those people here

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:00.777
that she cares about norms

00:16:00.777 --> 00:16:03.603
and about cleanliness and
about the country, basically.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.630
Then this team member will take a call.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:12.663
Someone will call her and
she will have a conversation,

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:18.783
but it's loud enough for
these people here to hear.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:24.920
And in the next step she will
drop a bunch of possessions

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.003
that she's cradling in a paper bag.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.780
The possessions are
groceries, oranges, actually,

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.790
the bag is torn at the bottom

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:37.170
and she's instructed
to make a sudden move,

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:40.230
drop her bag, which will
force her to move her arm

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.030
and then the oranges
will fall on the floor,

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.590
they'll disperse on the ground

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:47.850
and we observe whether these
guys help her pick them up.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:49.233
That's the setup, right?

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.860
I'm gonna go back into the details

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:55.230
and I'll tell you which are
the experimental dimensions

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:57.000
that we manipulate in a little bit.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:02.000
But first I'll show you the
video of this interaction.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.790
If I can figure out how to start it.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:06.813
There we go.

00:17:06.813 --> 00:17:09.960
This is Daniel Lo,
political science student.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:10.793
Dropped this cup.

00:17:10.793 --> 00:17:12.363
And that's Nestla, she's Turkish.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.250
You see this woman here
already talking about it.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.250
This woman here is working with us

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.500
and she's gonna record their reactions.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.840
And this one also, we
have two coders working

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:27.693
with the team, showed him off.

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:30.600
The three women are talking about him.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:31.700
This guy doesn't care.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.770
Now she's taking her call

00:17:38.086 --> 00:17:40.523
and she's talking to a friend
about coming to see her.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:44.730
Now she's instructed not
to pick up these oranges

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.580
for five seconds after she's dropped

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.480
so that we can give these
people a chance to pick them up.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:52.497
So this woman here and the other one

00:17:52.497 --> 00:17:54.930
who you saw was standing on that side

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:57.690
have recorded some information.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:02.460
First of all, they recorded
the number of bystanders

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.220
who were sitting, whether
they were sitting or not,

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:07.530
the gender of the bystanders

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.360
as subjective estimate of their age.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.800
Are they old or young?

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.440
Basically, can they get up
and help or not basically,

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.060
and some other things like is anybody

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.280
on a wheelchair, cane, all that?

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:20.250
And most importantly,
did anybody help, right?

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.130
So our main outcome in the
study is did anyone help

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.040
this woman pick up her oranges?

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:29.613
And also how many people help
is a secondary outcome, right?

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:34.470
So that's the intervention
basically, right?

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.960
And the key here is that
these people didn't know

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.883
that they were part of an experiment.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:46.670
And we believe that by
generating the situations

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:50.160
and collecting data this
way we get a more organic

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:55.020
and true estimate of
the willingness to help.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.560
Than it would be the case if
we just asked them in a survey,

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:00.030
would you help somebody in need?

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.620
In fact, we've done this

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.900
and there's a massive difference between

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.700
the stated willingness to help people

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.373
and the actual help that they provide.

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:12.990
Now what's the experiment
manipulating, right?

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.450
So there are three dimensions,

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:16.770
the identity of the confederate,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.100
the language of the phone
call and non-enforcement.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:23.010
So in the first treatment
dimension we manipulate

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.070
the descriptive characteristics
of the female confederate

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:27.573
who does the norm enforcement.

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:31.150
Here you see Stephanie, who's German

00:19:32.108 --> 00:19:34.710
and is our native control in this study,

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.290
and ML, who is Kurdish.

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:42.290
And she was actually a refugee in Turkey

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:45.000
and became a student.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.337
Now here you'll see that ML is wearing

00:19:50.683 --> 00:19:54.300
a hijab in one condition, no hijab,

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.650
she's basically dressed the same way

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:59.040
that Stephanie is dressed,
the way most women

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.950
of her age dressed in Germany students.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.730
And then here she's wearing
a big cross, you know,

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:09.930
like George Michael level cross, you know,

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.010
that's very visible
from far away to signal

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:15.150
that she's Christian, right?

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:18.540
So the idea here is that
social distance between ML

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.603
and Stephanie increases as
you go this direction, right?

00:20:21.603 --> 00:20:22.893
From right to left.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.390
And our hypothesis drawing
from social identity theory

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.700
which says that there is ingroup bias

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:33.670
whenever you highlight group differences

00:20:34.916 --> 00:20:36.420
between two groups is that

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.600
ML is gonna get much less
help in this condition here

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.060
compared to the other two conditions

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:46.140
where she's visibly less
distant from the native control.

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:49.140
But in all cases, we expect her

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:50.940
to receive less help than Stephanie.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.710
The second dimension that we manipulate is

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:57.180
norm enforcement, right?

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.340
So I won't go through
this whole thing again,

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.050
we'll focus on just on these two things.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:05.490
So as I mentioned, as
our female punisher will

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.070
sometimes enforce the norm,

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.990
tell the guy to pick up his trash.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:12.570
Other times she won't do
anything, she won't care.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:15.210
Basically she will just sit
there and look at the trash

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:16.770
and then mind her own business.

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:20.850
There's always enforcement
in these experiments, right?

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:25.200
So there's a another team
member who's also a woman

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.310
who comes in and tells...

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:29.490
So somebody always tells this
guy to pick up his trash.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:30.720
And we did that for two reasons.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:33.150
First, in Germany, somebody's
gonna tell this guy

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:35.400
to pick up his trash anyway.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:36.750
So we wanted to do it first

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.760
before there is intervention
by the bystanders.

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:43.590
And second, there is a
literature in psychology,

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:47.550
I'm not sure how robust the findings are

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:51.570
that there are these spillover effects

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.753
by observing positive
behavior, for example.

00:21:56.970 --> 00:21:58.770
So if you observe something for social,

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:02.580
somebody doing something nice,
then you might also respond

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.403
in a nice way on an entirely
different matter, you know?

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:11.100
So we wanted to keep the observation

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.750
of pro-social behavior constant

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:14.670
throughout these experiments.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:17.760
Because what we really
care about was whether

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:19.980
differences in the identity
of the person making

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:24.980
the experiment, making
the norm enforcement

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:28.140
really drive the results.

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:32.373
So we always had someone
enforcing them on, right? Yes.

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.780
- [Attendee 1] Maybe I'm
anticipating a little bit,

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:41.220
but would you run the experiment
also with different gender

00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:43.563
for the violator and the punishment?

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:47.640
- Yeah, so that's a
question that we get a lot

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:50.400
and it would be neat to do that.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.700
We didn't do that 'cause
there were 14 conditions

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:55.290
in this experiment.

00:22:55.290 --> 00:23:00.290
So then it would've just
blown up logistically.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.990
And that's actually the
reason why in these extensions

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.670
that I'm gonna talk about at the end,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:09.870
we use calibrated adaptations
of these interventions

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:14.700
in a survey based setting that
is much more cost effective

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.660
and allows us to check these variations.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:20.220
We chose women here

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:23.790
because we thought that it was less likely

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.580
that there's gonna be trouble, basically,

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:29.400
like if you had a woman littering

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:33.300
and a Muslim immigrant
man telling her off,

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:35.280
that could have been a
dangerous situation in some

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.710
of the towns that we went to, for example.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:41.550
But nobody is gonna be
violent against a woman who's

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:44.820
actually behaving in a pro-social
way and forcing the norm.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.140
It would would be pretty crazy actually.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:47.240
And it never happened.

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:51.690
So that was the reason why we did this.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:55.560
But obviously and I think
also helping behavior

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:59.610
tends to be higher baseline for women

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:01.380
than it would be for men.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:04.920
Some guy drop his oranges,
like pick them up yourself.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.920
So these were the reasons
why we we did that.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:09.750
Now the third dimension,

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:13.730
which I won't talk much about
in this presentation as part

00:24:18.269 --> 00:24:20.520
of the experiment nonetheless

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:22.230
is the language of the phone call, right?

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:27.230
So the woman who was holding the oranges

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.480
and either made the intervention

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:34.020
to enforce the norm or
not always took a call.

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:37.510
Actually the guy who was
leaving was the one calling her

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.960
and she spoke to him in either in German

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:45.330
or in Arabic or Turkish, okay?

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:48.510
And always had the same same script

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.600
that we gave her
basically, which was about,

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:53.010
oh, I'm excited to come see you.

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:53.997
It was something irrelevant basically.

00:24:53.997 --> 00:24:56.850
And the whole idea, it was

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.640
to make the call sound like
she's talking to her friend

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:02.160
and therefore if she's
talking to a friend in German,

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.140
that means that she has German friends

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:07.140
and therefore she's
assimilated to some extent

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.870
and her network includes Germans, right?

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:13.350
Which you would expect if it's a native,

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.030
but you might not expect if

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:19.860
she's hijab wearing Middle
Eastern woman, right?

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.083
And that could also influence behavior.

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.390
So that's the experiment.

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:27.180
Now where did we do this?

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:31.470
We did this in train stations
in 31 cities and towns

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:34.800
across three states in former East Germany

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.873
and in West Germany here, right?

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:43.640
So there were 34 confederates
across seven teams.

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:46.953
And these blue dots here
are the different cities.

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:52.260
And we were fortunate that
Germany has so many cities

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:54.990
with train stations that we can do this.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.660
I have pilots going on now
in Greece where I'm trying

00:25:57.660 --> 00:25:58.590
to do something like this

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:01.317
and it's like impossible basically.

00:26:01.317 --> 00:26:05.460
And so we're doing any like
bus stations and metro stations

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.490
and every public space you can imagine.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:11.220
And it's crazy complicated
logistically, right?

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:16.220
So we ran the intervention 1,614 times

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:19.270
and altogether that involved

00:26:20.189 --> 00:26:24.540
a little more than 7,000
bystanders and we had 14 treatment

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:27.150
and control conditions when
you combine the manipulations

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:29.040
for the three dimensions of.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:33.480
This is why we didn't have
male enforces, for example,

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:37.233
or consider other potential
interesting variations of this.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.850
So the main outcome as I mentioned

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:44.883
was did any bystander offer assistance?

00:26:46.048 --> 00:26:49.920
And there are logistical
details of the skid basically,

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:51.610
which I won't get into,

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.730
but you know, you can't
drop the orange here

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.010
and expect somebody from way
down there to run and get it,

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:57.900
although that happened once.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:01.350
This little girl sprinted
across, you know,

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:05.143
like 20 meters to try to help, you know,

00:27:05.143 --> 00:27:08.430
but it had to be within three
meters basically, right?

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:09.570
More or less.

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:12.510
And the oranges had to be on
the ground for a few seconds,

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:14.280
not so long that it becomes weird

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:17.400
that you haven't bent out
to pick them up yourself,

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:19.500
but long enough to give people a chance to

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:21.944
pick them up plus other,
there was like a long protocol

00:27:21.944 --> 00:27:26.823
about, you know, what counts
as help, what doesn't.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:30.540
The results I'm gonna show you here

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:33.930
referred to this binary indicator,

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:36.810
whether any bystander helped or not.

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:40.740
And in this experiment we
only did it in settings

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:43.050
where there were multiple
bystanders, right?

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.670
Never just one-on-one.

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:46.110
The next experiment
that I'm gonna show you,

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:49.380
we also did one-on-one interactions.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.690
And that kind of matters actually.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:55.020
So the hypothesis as I mentioned is that

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:56.430
that just flows straight out

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:58.850
of social psychological
theories of social identity

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:03.850
is that people will
receive less assistance

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:06.030
from bystanders if they're different.

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:08.940
And the more different
they are there was the less

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:10.050
assistance they will receive.

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:11.460
These are the first two.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:14.460
And the signaling cultural integration

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:16.710
by enforcing value of civic norms

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:20.380
will offset the bias that
we anticipate finding

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:26.070
against Muslim immigrant
enforcers, Muslim immigrants.

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:27.600
These are the main hypothesis.

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:32.440
So I won't talk about
any technical details

00:28:33.755 --> 00:28:35.460
in this presentation.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:37.127
I'll just show some bar graphs basically,

00:28:37.127 --> 00:28:39.693
but we can discuss details if you want.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:43.170
One of the main results is

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:47.820
that there is bias against
immigrants on the basis

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:49.413
of religion in Germany.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.710
The blue column here shows you

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.230
that providing help is
fairly common, right?

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:59.310
78% of the cases, there
was some help given

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:00.310
to the confederate

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:06.360
when the confederate was
a German native woman

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:10.380
and it wasn't much different
when it was an immigrant woman

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:12.540
wearing a cross or an immigrant woman

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:16.080
in the control condition
dressed in modern clothes

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.910
looking secular basically, right?

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:20.970
So these first three columns here suggest

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:23.100
that phenotypical differences,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.370
just looking like you're
not really German, basically

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:29.550
whatever that means or whatever
people think of German as

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:31.650
doesn't really matter
very much in Germany,

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:33.780
nor does the language actually matter,

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:35.610
which is very interesting

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:37.440
because in other countries in particular

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.990
in the United States, other
experiments have shown

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:41.310
that they matter a lot, right?

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:42.780
So there's an interesting experiment done

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:46.380
by Ryan Enos at Harvard
in Boston where he shows

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:49.990
that even just planting some confederates

00:29:51.119 --> 00:29:53.700
who are Latinos and
they look visibly Latino

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:58.700
in subway stations, generates
more hostile attitudes

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:02.340
towards affirmative action among
the commuters, for example.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:06.000
So even seeing someone
who's different racially

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:09.290
and listening to Spanish can
generate hostile responses

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:11.700
in the United States, not in Germany,

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:16.080
would suggest that they're
actually much more okay

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:17.890
with cultural differences
and ethnic difference,

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:19.920
which is what you would expect

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:24.090
when you have such a large
foreign worker population.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:25.948
Of course you would expect the same thing

00:30:25.948 --> 00:30:28.230
in the United States, but it's not.

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:30.990
Now this is interesting here.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:34.080
If you compare columns three and four,

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:39.080
you see the penalty that women incur

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:42.240
out of just showing that

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:43.840
they believe different things

00:30:44.778 --> 00:30:48.150
than the average German
believes by wearing the hijab.

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.820
So on average, women,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:54.330
immigrant women wearing a
hijab get help 66% of the time,

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:57.420
which is pretty good, you
know, more than half the time,

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:02.160
but much less, significantly
less than immigrant,

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:06.807
the same woman not wearing a hijab, right?

00:31:06.807 --> 00:31:11.340
Who gets help 76% of the time.
There's a 10% difference.

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:14.520
And that's the amount of the
penalty basically that you get

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:17.670
from being different in
this everyday interaction.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:20.220
You might say, well, 10% is not a lot.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.270
I don't know if it's a lot,
but it is what we picked up.

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:27.310
And it's also a fairly costless way

00:31:28.957 --> 00:31:30.960
of helping someone, right?

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:34.980
Like for someone who is
like not have a bad back

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:37.260
or something, it's nothing to bend down

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:38.310
and pick up an orange again,

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:40.770
especially if the orange hits your leg

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:42.690
or it's really closer to
you than somebody else.

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:47.200
So to withhold that
assistance systematically

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.540
towards someone on the basis
of what they look like,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.430
what you think they
believe is a significant

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:58.740
expression of hostility, we think,

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.020
and that's how we interpret this.

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:05.040
Now, does norm enforcement
change these results

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:06.480
in some way, right?

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.240
So we find that actually
bias, partially offset

00:32:11.903 --> 00:32:15.210
by shared norms, but it's not
completely eliminated, right?

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:20.210
So here, starting again
by German enforcer,

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:23.820
we see that they get helped
about 84% of the time,

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:26.290
significantly more than the previous

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:28.533
data that I showed you.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.160
And there is a statistically
significant difference

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:36.510
in the amount of help that
the German native gets

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:40.340
when she enforces a
norm versus not, right?

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:43.053
So she gets more help when
she enforces the norm.

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.480
When the immigrant wearing
a hijab enforces the norm,

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:52.533
she also gets much more
help than when she does not.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:57.323
There is now a 12.5% difference, right?

00:32:57.323 --> 00:33:00.700
So the hijab penalty increases when

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:10.970
there is also enforcement
versus non-enforcement.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:15.150
But an interesting finding
is that the amount of help

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:18.540
that the immigrant woman
wearing a hijab gets

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:23.540
when she enforces the
norm is basically the same

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:25.860
as the amount of help

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:27.810
that the German woman gets when she does

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:29.250
not enforce the norm.

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:33.070
So this is an example of

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:39.090
what you hear frequently being
said in the United States

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:40.950
and other countries,
which is that minorities

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:42.720
and immigrants have to work twice as hard

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.490
to get basically the
same kind of treatment

00:33:44.490 --> 00:33:46.320
that natives get, right?

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:51.320
So like these women get help the same way,

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.303
but this Middle Eastern woman here,

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:57.210
wearing hijab has gone out of her way

00:33:57.210 --> 00:34:01.050
to confront this big German
dude, you know, for being rude.

00:34:01.050 --> 00:34:04.020
Whereas this woman is
just white, you know,

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:05.820
and they get help the same.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:10.820
So while we see this increase,

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:15.450
right, from 60% to 73%

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:17.100
and it's statistically significant,

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:20.280
and therefore we know
that pro-social behavior

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:22.023
by immigrants is recognized,

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:25.890
there's still a difference
in difference basically.

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:27.960
And immigrant women, I'm sorry,

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:30.393
native women still get help more, right?

00:34:31.410 --> 00:34:33.180
This is not trivial though, right?

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:34.800
Because it could have been the case

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.570
that non-enforcement was
completely discounted

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:45.020
when done by an immigrant, right?

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:46.620
And we didn't find it.

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:48.910
So it could have been the case

00:34:49.831 --> 00:34:51.900
that these columns were equal, right?

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.870
So maybe people didn't,

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:57.120
and the fact that they're not suggests

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:01.170
that Germans actually
value non-enforcement

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:05.040
and rules more than the antipathy

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:08.250
that many of them feel
towards other people.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.140
Or it suggests that

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:15.150
the reason that they have
this antipathy might be

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:16.800
that they make assumptions
about the things

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.840
that immigrants actually believe, right?

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:20.730
So when you resolve that uncertainty

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:23.700
and you show them that they
actually share the same ideas

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:27.290
to some extent that the negative attitude

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:29.790
to immigrants isn't used.

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:31.170
So that's the main result.

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:32.100
- [Attendee 2] Can I ask you something?

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:32.933
- [Nicholas] Yes.

00:35:32.933 --> 00:35:33.840
- [Attendee 2] Now the third person,

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:36.090
when she tells the guy
to pick up his trash,

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:38.853
I assume she has to speak German?

00:35:38.853 --> 00:35:41.770
- In German with a slight accent.

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:43.353
Slight accent, yes.

00:35:45.570 --> 00:35:50.493
So now you might wonder, why?

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:55.980
So what drives this?

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:57.980
What is the mechanism, basically?

00:35:59.687 --> 00:36:02.820
What do people think about when
they see an immigrant woman

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.910
either reacting prosocially
by telling the guy

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:08.670
to pick up his trash
or not caring basically

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:13.080
when she stands right next
to somebody who litters.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:15.450
So to figure that out,

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:16.950
'cause we didn't have data on this

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:18.600
from our field experiment.

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:23.460
We ran a survey experiment in

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:27.600
maybe a few months
later, I believe in 2020,

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:31.900
where we showed videos from these

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:37.380
interventions and
pictures of litter streets

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:40.987
and collected data on basically
beliefs about littering,

00:36:40.987 --> 00:36:43.170
about why Germans litter,
why immigrants litter

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:44.547
who litters more and so on.

00:36:44.547 --> 00:36:47.040
And we found out that the
majority of Germans think

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:49.863
that immigrants litter more than Germans.

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:55.470
And when you ask them why
in an open-ended response

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:59.070
and you do a little word
cloud of their responses,

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:01.920
you see that this word
homeland and respect

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:06.750
and habit is what comes up more frequently

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:08.880
in spontaneous responses about, you know,

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:11.122
why immigrants litter,

00:37:11.122 --> 00:37:12.840
they litter because they're not from here,

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:14.490
this isn't their homeland,
they don't really care,

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:17.640
they don't have the habit
not to litter, right?

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:20.100
Which suggest that it's all about respect

00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:25.100
and showing respect and affinity
for the new country, right?

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:30.060
And that is what registers,

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:34.050
when they observe an immigrant
woman enforcing the norm,

00:37:34.050 --> 00:37:35.400
that she actually does care

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:38.490
and she respects the country
that she lives in now

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:40.620
and therefore she can be considered part

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:42.120
of the citizen ingroup.

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:45.090
But on the basis of this
experiment that is not enough

00:37:45.090 --> 00:37:46.680
to eliminate all bias, right?

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:48.500
Because there's still a
difference in difference

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:49.900
in the results I showed you.

00:37:52.473 --> 00:37:55.890
So far we've learned
that Germans discriminate

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:59.850
against Muslims in everyday
interactions to some extent

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:01.647
that ethnolinguistic differences

00:38:01.647 --> 00:38:04.590
and phenotypical differences
don't really matter.

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:09.540
And therefore, this discrimination

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:13.200
must be driven by what people read

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.300
into these descriptive differences, right?

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.943
What do they think the
hijab means, for example.

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:22.140
And that these perceptions
of normative differences

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:25.960
with immigrants, that underlie the bias

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:29.876
can be manipulated and
when you change them,

00:38:29.876 --> 00:38:32.176
that can reduce the bias
but not eliminate it.

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:36.900
So what are the mechanisms
underlying this again, right?

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:40.110
So social psychological
theory in particular

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:42.543
common ingroup identity model,

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:47.350
which was developed

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:51.370
by Gartner and Dovidio,
who is here at Yale

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:56.340
suggests some possible
explanations and mechanisms.

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:58.530
Two mechanisms that they have suggested

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:01.380
is rec-categorization and
de-categorization, right?

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:04.140
So the idea is that you
can reduce prejudice

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:08.550
or someone else if you can
redefine that person as a member

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:10.140
of a common ingroup, right?

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:12.600
Rather than a member
of a hostile outgroup.

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:16.230
What can this common ingroup
be when we're dealing

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:17.270
with natives and immigrants?

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:19.170
It can't be a national ingroup,

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:21.120
but it could be a citizen ingroup, right?

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:23.220
So the idea that we had here is

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:25.230
that if everyone behaves the same way

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:28.740
and they show that they
share the same ideas about

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:32.220
standards of appropriate civic
behavior, they can be part

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:35.070
of a shared citizen ingroup.

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:38.610
And that is the way that
they can be recategorized

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:41.520
as an ingroup member
from an outgroup member

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:46.290
or a different mechanism, is
that they can decategorized

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:49.950
as a member of a hostile outgroup

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:51.210
by considering these people

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:54.210
as not typical members of
that hostile outgroup, right?

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:57.030
So you could look at the
person, the immigrant woman

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:59.220
with a hijab who's enforcing the norm

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:03.600
and think of her as a good
citizen, not as a Muslim.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:06.330
Or you can look at her and
say, yeah, yes, she's a Muslim,

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:07.800
but she's a good Muslim,

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:10.230
not a bad Muslim like the
others, basically, right?

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:12.270
So the first would be recategorization,

00:40:12.270 --> 00:40:15.571
the second would be
decategorization mechanism.

00:40:15.571 --> 00:40:20.100
So, and we wanted to
test these mechanisms.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:22.950
And a third thing that we wanted to test

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:25.620
is whether the benefit
of prosocial behavior

00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:29.910
that is generated by
observing norm enforcement

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.550
by one person generalizes to the group,

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:36.063
or is it restricted to
the person itself, right?

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:41.400
So to do that we ran
another survey experiment

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:45.180
which consisted of showing
people videos, right?

00:40:45.180 --> 00:40:46.570
Now, this video here

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:52.050
was filmed by us and it was

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.930
as if randomly captured
on the phone, right?

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.930
So Mattias was sitting here on a bench

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:03.150
and he was taking a video with his phone

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:04.860
of the surroundings in the back, right?

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:06.630
And he was saying, oh look,
they finished the construction

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:10.410
at the station, you know the
way people do all the time

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:14.070
and then they post the stuff
on YouTube or TikTok, right?

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:15.510
And then as he was doing that,

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:18.030
these people walk into the frame, right?

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:21.630
And then we repeated this intervention,

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:22.800
not the norm enforcement,

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:27.270
just the littering, I'm
sorry, not the oranges part,

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:31.680
just a norm violation and
the enforcement, right?

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:34.470
So this guy who's pretty huge actually,

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:36.820
and he wouldn't want to
tell him off, you know,

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:42.390
without police around you
or something, drops his cup.

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:46.920
And then this woman who's
pretty small actually tells him

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:50.640
to pick up his trash or not, you know?

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.950
So in one iteration she just sits there

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.140
and they're actually, her friends are

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:56.703
sitting just off that frame.

00:41:57.661 --> 00:41:59.430
There were some versions
in which there were a

00:41:59.430 --> 00:42:02.703
group of four and other
versions in which she was alone.

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:07.800
So we have an experiment again in which

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:11.820
norm violation is either sanctioned

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:16.650
or not sanctioned by a woman who is either

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:20.550
a Muslim woman wearing a hijab
or a German woman, right?

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:24.990
So we have videos of all of
these different conditions

00:42:24.990 --> 00:42:28.710
and we upload them on
YouTube in an account

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:31.683
and channel that we created,

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:35.430
we let them sit there for a while

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:40.430
and then we use the link in
a survey instrument saying,

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:43.260
look, we wanna know

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:47.160
what you think about these
situations which we found online,

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:50.430
including this video that
we happen to find online.

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:53.670
So it's as if it's a real intervention

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:57.090
that we capture in the field.

00:42:57.090 --> 00:43:01.120
And we implement the survey

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:07.383
in a sample of 2500 German
adults in November, 2020.

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:11.400
And we are interested in
measuring responses using

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:12.540
this item basically.

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:17.540
So after they view the video,
they're asked to consider

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:21.840
whether the person in the video is similar

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:23.640
and how similar that person is

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.070
to an outstanding German citizen, right?

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:28.857
Are they 0% similar, 20% similar, 40%,

00:43:28.857 --> 00:43:31.920
and so on until identical
basically, right?

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:34.210
This is a common way of measuring

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:38.053
perceived similarity in
the American politics,

00:43:38.053 --> 00:43:39.813
literature and economics.

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:44.940
And we call this the
recategorization item.

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:50.350
Because the idea is that
if the person in question

00:43:51.330 --> 00:43:53.520
is an immigrant wearing a hijab

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:56.193
and you think that she's
a hundred percent similar

00:43:56.193 --> 00:43:58.200
to a good outstanding citizen,

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:01.740
then you're thinking
about her as a citizen

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:06.450
rather than as a foreigner, right?

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:09.600
Because an upstanding German
citizen would've done exactly

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.270
what a person did

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:15.900
in the iterations where they
enforced the norm, right?

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.180
So I won't show you all the analysis,

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:20.190
I'll just give you a
snapshot of the results.

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.490
And the results are that
norm enforces are seen

00:44:23.490 --> 00:44:27.210
as more similar to good Germans citizens,

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:29.527
regardless of whether they
were a hijab or no hijab.

00:44:29.527 --> 00:44:32.670
The hijab no longer
matters basically, right?

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:34.660
So we have a very large shift

00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:41.283
from zero to a hundred, although
it's not a hundred, okay?

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:43.920
And norm enforcers,

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:47.550
so we have evidence of the
recategorization mechanism

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:50.940
and norm enforcers are
seen as not typical members

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:52.560
of the group, right?

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:55.260
When the normal enforcer
is an immigrant, right?

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:58.660
So a Muslim woman enforcing the norm

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:03.600
is seen as more similar
to a good German citizen

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:06.690
and because she's not a
typical Muslim, right?

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:10.710
So these are the main results here

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:15.090
and the benefit of norm enforcement,

00:45:15.090 --> 00:45:18.690
seeing this norm enforcement
in the video doesn't change

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:21.180
your views of Muslims as
a group in general, right?

00:45:21.180 --> 00:45:24.450
So yes, you recognize that this one person

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:25.920
did something good.

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:29.191
Yes, it's closer to a good German citizen,

00:45:29.191 --> 00:45:30.330
(indistinct) as a whole

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:31.770
are still as bad or as good

00:45:31.770 --> 00:45:33.903
as we thought about previously, right?

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:37.767
So there is evidence of
both recategorization

00:45:37.767 --> 00:45:42.767
and decategorization, and
this one shot intervention

00:45:44.460 --> 00:45:47.910
that we did in the video
doesn't really change

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:49.770
perceptions of the group in general.

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:52.830
That might also be true, is probably true

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:54.330
in the field as well.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:58.050
So seeing somebody doing
something good in the field

00:45:58.050 --> 00:46:00.390
who's from a different
outgroup is probably not

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:03.033
gonna change your worldview
about everybody else.

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:08.640
But our hypothesis is that
if you were to see examples

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:11.040
of such pro-social behavior
over and over again

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:12.750
by many different people everywhere,

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:15.450
that should over time change your views.

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:17.163
But that's still untested.

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:22.837
So now you might ask,

00:46:22.837 --> 00:46:27.087
"Well what about other norms?"

00:46:28.013 --> 00:46:31.443
Okay, so we talked about littering,

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:36.420
but littering is kind of a generic,

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:38.880
not littering is a generic exhibit norm.

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:43.830
Everybody kind of feels
the same way about it.

00:46:43.830 --> 00:46:46.560
It's hard to find someone who's
gonna tell you in a survey,

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:48.260
yes, I'd like to litter, you know,

00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:51.570
but maybe the reason that we find

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:55.110
that the diff in diff still there is

00:46:55.110 --> 00:46:58.410
because enforcing a non littering norm

00:46:58.410 --> 00:47:00.120
has shallow effects, right?

00:47:00.120 --> 00:47:02.410
You'll give somebody the benefit

00:47:06.173 --> 00:47:08.970
of saying that you feel a
little closer to that person

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:10.232
because they enforce the norm,

00:47:10.232 --> 00:47:12.150
but it's not gonna change
the way you feel about

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:14.340
that person massively.

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:19.020
What about if we focus
on group specific norms?

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:22.410
And by that we mean civic
norms that affect your subgroup

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:24.570
of the population more, right?

00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:27.210
To do these generate stronger effects.

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:30.090
And here we focus on
gender equity and norms

00:47:30.090 --> 00:47:33.420
about gender roles in particular.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:37.647
We hypothesize

00:47:37.647 --> 00:47:42.647
that the reason that women
wearing a hijab in Germany

00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:45.690
get penalized and discriminated against

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:48.630
in this public settings is
because people make assumptions

00:47:48.630 --> 00:47:51.240
about their beliefs
regarding gender equality.

00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:54.203
'Cause they see a hijab,
they assume that the woman

00:47:54.203 --> 00:47:59.010
is regressive vis-a-vis
gender equality norms.

00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:00.570
And they punish her for that

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:02.760
by not helping her when she needs help.

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:05.490
What if we actually
were able to communicate

00:48:05.490 --> 00:48:09.513
that woman's beliefs about
gender roles in the same way

00:48:09.513 --> 00:48:11.670
that we did in the previous experiment?

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:13.890
Would that change things in the field?

00:48:13.890 --> 00:48:16.440
So we designed and another experiment

00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:18.030
that tested exactly that, right?

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:21.550
So our hypothesis is that
discrimination against Muslims

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:24.090
is likely driven by beliefs

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:26.310
that they have regressive
ideas about gender.

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:28.890
That is a big topic
actually in immigration,

00:48:28.890 --> 00:48:30.720
studies of immigration in Europe,

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:33.700
gender and the role of women

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:38.190
in Muslim societies versus
Christian societies is a point

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:40.473
of conflict and debate.

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:44.250
So we confirm actually

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:47.400
that this assumption is
widely shared in Germany

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:48.840
in a standard survey.

00:48:48.840 --> 00:48:49.837
So we tell them,

00:48:49.837 --> 00:48:52.020
"What do you think about
hijab", for example?

00:48:52.020 --> 00:48:55.710
And repression is what
comes immediately as

00:48:55.710 --> 00:49:00.420
the most commonly mentioned
word in this survey.

00:49:00.420 --> 00:49:03.510
So we said, "Well what
if Muslim women signal

00:49:03.510 --> 00:49:07.230
that they actually have
progressive ideas about German?"

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:09.390
Does that reduce discrimination

00:49:09.390 --> 00:49:12.270
and does that make the
hijab just an irrelevant

00:49:12.270 --> 00:49:16.530
cultural symbol that they
wear for other reasons, right?

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:20.790
Rather than to signal political ideology

00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:24.960
that is contrary to the
dominant view in Germany.

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:26.910
And we did that with
another field experiment

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:29.520
the following summer, summer of 2019,

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:30.900
which had the same idea,

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:32.820
but we dropped lemons this time.

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:34.800
So rather than oranges, right?

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:37.320
So here is Nestle,

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:38.880
she is the same woman that you saw before.

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:40.320
She loved being part of this whole thing.

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:43.740
So she re-upped this the
second she lived in Cologne

00:49:43.740 --> 00:49:46.803
and she was a political
science student, she's Turkish.

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:50.370
So she approaches a group of people

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:53.610
who could be standing
or talking or sitting

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:55.350
and she takes a call, right?

00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:58.380
And in the call she
will reveal her beliefs

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:00.000
about gender equality.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:01.260
And I'll tell you how.

00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:04.440
So we manipulate two dimensions here.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:07.049
The first is the identity
of the confederate.

00:50:07.049 --> 00:50:10.170
Just like in the previous one,
she can be a German native.

00:50:10.170 --> 00:50:11.193
This is Charlotte.

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:15.450
She was in one of these German teams

00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:18.633
and this is Raneem who's a Syrian refugee.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:22.170
And here is Raneem in
the control condition

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:23.670
wearing no hijab.

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:26.340
And here is Raneem wearing
hijab basically, right?

00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:29.130
So we have three
conditions, immigrant hijab,

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:31.923
immigrant no hijab and native.

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:35.463
And these women dressed,

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:40.290
depending on the way that the condition

00:50:40.290 --> 00:50:44.370
that they were assigned to
suggest they should be dressed,

00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:45.960
would approach this group

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:48.310
and have a conversation in which

00:50:51.666 --> 00:50:54.720
they reveal their views
on gender by saying

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:56.460
one of these three things.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:51:00.540
So in one condition she will speak,

00:51:00.540 --> 00:51:02.670
she's always speaking to a friend of hers

00:51:02.670 --> 00:51:05.470
and she's expressing her

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:10.650
emotional state about a
decision that her sister took

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:14.100
to either take a job or not, right?

00:51:14.100 --> 00:51:16.830
So in their regressive condition message,

00:51:16.830 --> 00:51:18.270
she says, "Hi, thanks for calling back.

00:51:18.270 --> 00:51:21.510
I'm really mad. My sister's
a terrible mother."

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:22.980
There's a word for that in German

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:25.350
that people really pay attention to

00:51:25.350 --> 00:51:28.500
when they listen to it, like raven mother.

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:31.950
Although I don't know if
ravens are bad mothers or not.

00:51:31.950 --> 00:51:34.080
She prefers to work instead of looking

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:36.180
after her children and
her husband at home.

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:37.860
And then there's a pause

00:51:37.860 --> 00:51:40.380
and then she says, "I think
that as a woman she stay at home

00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:41.730
and look after her family.

00:51:41.730 --> 00:51:44.070
I've never been so mad
since we moved to Germany."

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:47.100
We added this sentence to make clear

00:51:47.100 --> 00:51:49.740
that these people were actually immigrants

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:50.970
or recent immigrants

00:51:50.970 --> 00:51:53.920
because that wasn't actually
clear in the first experiment.

00:51:55.110 --> 00:51:58.920
And then in the progressive
message, it's the same setting.

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:00.870
The sister takes a job or not,

00:52:00.870 --> 00:52:03.270
but she's happy about that, right?

00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:07.170
So a woman, "I'm really happy,
very proud of my sister,

00:52:07.170 --> 00:52:08.490
she decided to go to work.

00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:10.510
She shouldn't just stay at home to look

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:12.360
after her children and husband, right?"

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:13.860
And the control condition,

00:52:13.860 --> 00:52:17.040
she basically has similar
length conversation

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:19.260
with a friend about
some irrelevant subject.

00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:21.585
Oh, I'm coming to see you, I'm excited,

00:52:21.585 --> 00:52:24.063
blah, blah, blah, right?

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:27.750
- [Attendee 1] And what
does the control person say

00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:29.703
then regarding Germany?

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:37.347
- She says, I'm happy to come to see you.

00:52:39.270 --> 00:52:40.650
And then something happened in her life.

00:52:40.650 --> 00:52:42.059
I can't remember what we said now.

00:52:42.059 --> 00:52:45.243
And then it ends with
basically the same thing.

00:52:47.160 --> 00:52:51.690
I'm happy, so she's happy in the control,

00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:53.130
happy in the progressive

00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:54.993
and mad in the regressive.

00:52:57.240 --> 00:53:01.590
Now the data collection
here lasted five weeks,

00:53:01.590 --> 00:53:06.590
25 cities, in 26 train
stations in NRW, Saxony,

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:07.980
and Lower Saxony.

00:53:07.980 --> 00:53:12.630
And here we repeated this
situation 1,830 times

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:14.880
and we had almost 4,000 bystanders, right?

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:16.530
Again, within three meters.

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:19.920
In a pilot the spring before,

00:53:19.920 --> 00:53:22.470
Mathias and I went to Frankfurt

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:25.470
and we ran this like about 250 times

00:53:25.470 --> 00:53:27.900
and we then had a team of people come

00:53:27.900 --> 00:53:29.910
and interview everybody who was around.

00:53:29.910 --> 00:53:33.360
So we established that
in like 98% of the times

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:34.920
they noticed the confederate.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:37.170
96% of the time they noticed the call.

00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:39.630
And 86% of the times they
could tell you exactly

00:53:39.630 --> 00:53:42.840
what she said, even when
they were wearing headphones,

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:44.100
which is pretty weird I think.

00:53:44.100 --> 00:53:46.846
But it is what it is.

00:53:46.846 --> 00:53:51.270
You know, so very nosy people
who wanna know what's going on

00:53:51.270 --> 00:53:54.900
around them, which works for
this experiment quite well.

00:53:54.900 --> 00:53:59.880
So here we collect data on
how many bystanders there were

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:03.270
and we collect, in this
instance we were better at it.

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:05.943
So we had individual level data.

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:09.900
We had up to seven bystanders.

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:11.493
And then for each bystander,

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:15.600
what's the gender of that person?

00:54:15.600 --> 00:54:17.940
Were they wearing headphones?

00:54:17.940 --> 00:54:20.440
What is the subjective
estimate of the age bracket

00:54:21.300 --> 00:54:23.130
or the potential of immigrant background?

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:25.440
That's all subjective obviously.

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:28.077
And did this bystander offer assistance?

00:54:28.077 --> 00:54:32.070
And that allows us to analyze
the data both by iteration,

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:33.810
did anybody help in that iteration or not?

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:37.617
But also to say something about
or women help more or not.

00:54:37.617 --> 00:54:41.520
And that was useful because
after the intervention,

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:42.660
we had a survey,

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:45.870
another team came in a few minutes later

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:48.870
and said, "Oh, we're from
this university in this town,

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.540
we're running the survey, doing the study.

00:54:51.540 --> 00:54:55.407
Can we do a survey about
social issues in Germany?"

00:54:56.280 --> 00:54:59.550
It was extremely hard
to get people to say yes

00:54:59.550 --> 00:55:00.840
to that unfortunately.

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:02.670
And we didn't really get great data.

00:55:02.670 --> 00:55:07.350
We tried everything,
tried giving away $2,000.

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:10.050
We tried this weird system
where we gave them these cards

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:13.140
and we could track them so they
can actually take the survey

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:14.370
later when they wanted to

00:55:14.370 --> 00:55:17.160
and they never worked well basically.

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:20.310
But we have some data
that we can actually match

00:55:20.310 --> 00:55:22.200
with the individual level data

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:25.983
and that gives us some
interesting findings actually.

00:55:27.210 --> 00:55:29.020
So the first result is that

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:32.640
we replicate the finding
from the previous summer,

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:34.650
which is that there is bias

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:36.570
on the basis for religion, right?

00:55:36.570 --> 00:55:40.927
We have the average level of
help is about the same, 76%.

00:55:40.927 --> 00:55:45.927
It was 78% in 2018 for the native.

00:55:45.960 --> 00:55:48.660
And it's basically the same
for the immigrant control,

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:52.517
no discrimination there, but it's 67.5%

00:55:54.390 --> 00:55:56.160
for the immigrant with a hijab.

00:55:56.160 --> 00:55:58.440
And that difference is
statistically significant.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:00.153
Exactly the same as before.

00:56:02.040 --> 00:56:04.080
And here's the interesting stuff here.

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:08.013
So lots of dimensions here to consider.

00:56:09.060 --> 00:56:12.450
The first dimension is just
compare the bluish columns

00:56:12.450 --> 00:56:14.130
with the reddish columns.

00:56:14.130 --> 00:56:15.900
And we see that the bluish columns,

00:56:15.900 --> 00:56:18.840
which are all about natives,
the different conditions,

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:21.810
progressive, neutral or
aggressive in the natives

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:23.370
are all about the same.

00:56:23.370 --> 00:56:26.370
Whereas there are
differences in immigrants.

00:56:26.370 --> 00:56:31.020
So basically what these native women say

00:56:31.020 --> 00:56:34.560
doesn't really influence
whether they get help or not.

00:56:34.560 --> 00:56:37.980
So whereas for immigrants,

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:41.190
it matters quite a bit whether
they appear to be progressive

00:56:41.190 --> 00:56:42.600
or regressive, right?

00:56:42.600 --> 00:56:47.220
And the progressive woman wearing a hijab

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:50.100
gets helped just as much.

00:56:50.100 --> 00:56:51.960
There's no statistically
significant difference

00:56:51.960 --> 00:56:56.340
as the German woman in
any condition, right?

00:56:56.340 --> 00:56:58.990
So there's no longer any bias

00:56:59.880 --> 00:57:02.820
towards Muslim women when
they appear to be regressive.

00:57:02.820 --> 00:57:05.820
And that result is driven
by women, German women.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:09.510
German women no longer
discriminate against Muslim women

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:13.080
when the Muslim woman
appears to be progressive.

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:15.660
There is a significant penalty

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:19.890
to wearing hijab when it
is associated in confirming

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:23.493
the priors that this woman
is regressive, right?

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:27.210
But there is no analogous penalty

00:57:27.210 --> 00:57:29.270
to being regressive if
you're German, right?

00:57:29.270 --> 00:57:32.010
So that in and of itself
can be interpreted

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:34.230
as a subtle sign of bias, right?

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:37.800
So basically white
German women are allowed

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:41.610
to have whatever views they
have and you'll still help them.

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:45.330
But the Muslim women, that's
not the case basically, right?

00:57:45.330 --> 00:57:48.840
So you help them only if they show you

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:51.630
that they believe the same
thing that you believe.

00:57:51.630 --> 00:57:55.950
Now men are not affected at all

00:57:55.950 --> 00:57:58.230
by this intervention, right?

00:57:58.230 --> 00:57:59.490
It doesn't change their reaction.

00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:03.310
In fact, they discriminate
a little more against women

00:58:05.058 --> 00:58:07.650
in the progressive condition

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:12.510
and the regressive condition
relative to the control.

00:58:12.510 --> 00:58:14.260
Like whenever they talk basically

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:18.266
it upsets them a little bit
(audience laughing)

00:58:18.266 --> 00:58:20.880
and they help them a little less,

00:58:20.880 --> 00:58:24.333
but it's only a very, very
small annoyance penalty.

00:58:27.353 --> 00:58:30.690
So, but they don't care
basically if the Muslim women are

00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:34.270
regressive or so there's
something else that's driving male

00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:37.590
discrimination against Muslim women.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:41.940
And the third finding,

00:58:41.940 --> 00:58:44.340
which comes from the
individual level data,

00:58:44.340 --> 00:58:49.340
is that this entire set of
results comes from secular.

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:54.540
There's a 50 percentage points
difference in the help rate

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:57.780
among women who declare to be religious

00:58:57.780 --> 00:58:59.820
and women who declare to be atheist.

00:58:59.820 --> 00:59:03.990
And the secular women
in Germany are the ones

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:07.290
who appreciate the progressive message.

00:59:07.290 --> 00:59:11.670
Christian women do not
in fact sometimes punish

00:59:11.670 --> 00:59:15.180
the German women for that position, right?

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:17.770
Just as in the previous
example, we follow up

00:59:18.990 --> 00:59:23.170
with a survey experiment to
figure out exactly why do people

00:59:27.990 --> 00:59:30.330
discriminate against
the hijab wearing women.

00:59:30.330 --> 00:59:34.050
And we ran a survey
showing them a video of one

00:59:34.050 --> 00:59:36.540
of our confederates when she wears a hijab

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:37.890
who drops her oranges in front

00:59:37.890 --> 00:59:40.680
of these German people
and nobody helps her.

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:41.513
And we ask them,

00:59:41.513 --> 00:59:44.280
"Why do you think she
doesn't get any help?"

00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:47.820
And the most common
explanation, like at 65%

00:59:47.820 --> 00:59:50.520
of the people give you
immediately is that it's

00:59:50.520 --> 00:59:55.023
because they think that she does not think

00:59:55.023 --> 00:59:56.730
that women are equal

00:59:56.730 --> 00:59:58.440
and they think women should
be subordinate, right?

00:59:58.440 --> 01:00:00.160
So the whole idea here is that

01:00:01.770 --> 01:00:05.430
when people look at the
hijab as a symbol of Islam,

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:08.310
they assume that it's a
symbol of political Islam

01:00:08.310 --> 01:00:12.640
and that it means that it represents

01:00:13.770 --> 01:00:17.490
a set of politically
regressive beliefs, which can,

01:00:17.490 --> 01:00:20.010
if you allow a lot of Muslims to come in

01:00:20.010 --> 01:00:23.940
and live in Germany, shape
the politics of the country

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:27.990
and causing backtracking on
advances in women's rights

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:28.867
that have occurred over time, right?

01:00:28.867 --> 01:00:30.900
So that's what as the explanation

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.270
that we believe drives
the results that we find.

01:00:33.270 --> 01:00:34.980
And men are not responsive to this

01:00:34.980 --> 01:00:37.770
because they don't really care
that much about the progress

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:39.660
that is made in women race

01:00:39.660 --> 01:00:42.060
because they don't
really benefit from this.

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:45.000
Just to close basically

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:48.000
because I told you, I'll
tell you where this is going.

01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:52.230
So we're interested in these questions.

01:00:52.230 --> 01:00:53.830
You know, does race play a role?

01:00:55.170 --> 01:00:57.540
What if the enforcer is male?

01:00:57.540 --> 01:01:00.420
What if we're talking about
other norms, you know,

01:01:00.420 --> 01:01:03.150
not just littering and gender equity.

01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:05.407
It's extremely complicated
to do this in the field.

01:01:05.407 --> 01:01:07.260
And these experiments

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:10.320
are massively complicated logistically.

01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:12.960
So we were in the field from
like seven in the morning

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:16.110
until seven, eight at night every day

01:01:16.110 --> 01:01:18.090
for five, six weeks at a time.

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:20.190
And we have 55 people working

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:25.113
across the country basically for months.

01:01:26.130 --> 01:01:28.650
So it's not possible to look at these

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:29.880
in a field experiment setting.

01:01:29.880 --> 01:01:32.910
So what we're doing now is we're creating

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:35.940
calibrated versions of these interventions

01:01:35.940 --> 01:01:37.593
in survey experiments.

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:42.960
And I can tell you a little
more about how that is done.

01:01:42.960 --> 01:01:46.230
And the idea is to run survey experiments

01:01:46.230 --> 01:01:49.410
in all European union countries
with very large samples

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:51.690
of about 4,000 people per country.

01:01:51.690 --> 01:01:55.560
But we measure first try to find out

01:01:55.560 --> 01:01:59.730
what norms do you these
European citizens care about

01:01:59.730 --> 01:02:03.990
among a larger set of norms
that define good citizenship,

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:05.800
rank order these norms

01:02:06.900 --> 01:02:11.250
and create norm hierarchies
for every country.

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:14.340
And then see whether people
apply these hierarchies

01:02:14.340 --> 01:02:16.980
consistently to evaluate natives

01:02:16.980 --> 01:02:19.173
and immigrants in every country.

01:02:20.700 --> 01:02:23.880
And that involves basically
a two stage process

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:28.170
where we elicit beliefs
about the norms that matter,

01:02:28.170 --> 01:02:31.230
and perceptions about what
other people in this countries

01:02:31.230 --> 01:02:35.010
think is the right way of behaving, right?

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:37.980
And that will tell us whether
there's an implicit consensus

01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:42.750
in the European Union, perhaps
as a result of being part

01:02:42.750 --> 01:02:45.660
of the union about what it
means to be a good citizen

01:02:45.660 --> 01:02:49.260
and whether those norms of
good citizenship are applied

01:02:49.260 --> 01:02:52.473
to judge natives and immigrants equally.

01:02:54.030 --> 01:02:56.853
So just to wrap up,

01:02:57.720 --> 01:02:59.160
we learned in this book

01:02:59.160 --> 01:03:01.890
that there are cultural
differences cause bias

01:03:01.890 --> 01:03:06.000
that coercive assimilation,
you know, changing your name

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:09.420
or dropping the hijab
might not be necessary

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:11.910
if you can disconnect

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:15.600
what these symbols
actually mean politically

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:19.920
from the religious or other
cultural significance.

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:23.130
But we also learned that norm enforcement

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:27.150
and adhering to valued norms
might actually not be enough

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:28.773
to eliminate all bias.

01:03:31.200 --> 01:03:35.040
And that multiculturalism has some limits.

01:03:35.040 --> 01:03:38.580
So you can overcome
descriptive differences,

01:03:38.580 --> 01:03:41.730
but natives will resist abandoning norms

01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:43.380
and ideas that define their identities

01:03:43.380 --> 01:03:45.060
that they've developed over time

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:48.210
and they've been socialized
in for the benefit of

01:03:48.210 --> 01:03:52.590
accommodating newcomers and
the values that they represent.

01:03:52.590 --> 01:03:55.980
So there are some policy
implications of this research

01:03:55.980 --> 01:03:58.360
as well that we're trying to uncover

01:03:59.850 --> 01:04:01.920
now in the work that is ongoing.

01:04:01.920 --> 01:04:06.920
And one result is that
there is no easy way of

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:09.900
creating like some public
information campaign

01:04:09.900 --> 01:04:14.307
that will work for everybody
and reduce bias, you know, so,

01:04:14.307 --> 01:04:15.990
and that's unfortunate,

01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:20.220
but the multidimensionality of
social identities basically,

01:04:20.220 --> 01:04:23.490
and the complicated way in
which the different identities

01:04:23.490 --> 01:04:26.123
intersect, make it impossible for us

01:04:26.123 --> 01:04:28.950
to have a single measures that
will work well for everyone.

01:04:28.950 --> 01:04:31.650
And we saw that in the
gender experiment where

01:04:31.650 --> 01:04:35.160
the message that these women
are actually progressive works

01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:38.580
very well on women, some
women, secular women,

01:04:38.580 --> 01:04:40.650
but not at all among Christian women

01:04:40.650 --> 01:04:43.260
and men are indifferent basically, right?

01:04:43.260 --> 01:04:46.530
So imagine if you had a
public information campaign

01:04:46.530 --> 01:04:48.960
basically that highlighted some feature

01:04:48.960 --> 01:04:50.640
of an immigrant population.

01:04:50.640 --> 01:04:52.590
It could work for a subset of population

01:04:52.590 --> 01:04:54.450
but not for the entire population.

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:55.860
So it's very hard

01:04:55.860 --> 01:04:58.800
to find a scalable public
information campaign

01:04:58.800 --> 01:05:01.290
that can just eliminate prejudices

01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:03.183
and misperceptions just like that.

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:06.600
And as a first step for a policy design

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:11.070
that actually address
these issues, we think that

01:05:11.070 --> 01:05:13.470
we need to collect more
information about first,

01:05:13.470 --> 01:05:15.030
what are the priors that people have

01:05:15.030 --> 01:05:16.740
about different immigrant groups?

01:05:16.740 --> 01:05:19.800
How do they expect them to behave?

01:05:19.800 --> 01:05:22.620
Which norms do they
think they will violate

01:05:22.620 --> 01:05:24.270
and which they will adhere to?

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:27.160
And then how much do people
care about the specific norms

01:05:28.484 --> 01:05:31.140
that immigrants are likely to violate?

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:33.240
And once we have that information,

01:05:33.240 --> 01:05:36.150
we can then create citizenship classes

01:05:36.150 --> 01:05:38.190
that can inform not just immigrants,

01:05:38.190 --> 01:05:41.133
but also natives about this.

01:05:42.303 --> 01:05:45.360
They can share the information
with both native populations

01:05:45.360 --> 01:05:49.540
and immigrant populations leading to

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:52.500
fewer misperceptions about the degree

01:05:52.500 --> 01:05:55.210
to which immigrants are non violators

01:05:56.460 --> 01:05:59.440
and pushing both groups
towards a convergence

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:03.070
in their public behaviors

01:06:05.370 --> 01:06:08.100
and adherence to the set of
norms of good citizenship

01:06:08.100 --> 01:06:11.580
that could allow this
recategorization mechanism

01:06:11.580 --> 01:06:14.160
that we described to take call basically.

01:06:14.160 --> 01:06:17.703
So I'll stop here.
(audience clapping)

01:06:20.507 --> 01:06:24.170
- [Attendee 1] There is a
question on line if you for now.

01:06:24.170 --> 01:06:27.410
If you join me thanking
Professor Sambanis for this...

